{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 34, :comments ["<b>For how shall I go up, etc.</b> Meaning, without the reason that he stated, namely ״וְחָטָאתִי״ etc., ״אֵיךְ אֶעֱלֶה״ etc. Further, it may be that he gives a reason for what he said at first (Genesis 44:16) ״הִנְנוּ עֲבָדִים גַּם אֲנַחְנוּ״ etc. — ״כִּי אֵיךְ אֶעֱלֶה״ etc. ״פֶּן אֶרְאֶה בָרָע״ — for when they do not all go up, he will not see, etc." "<b>Further,</b> it may intend to respond to the argument of the wise woman that Joab sent to David (II Samuel 14:7), for the family has risen to kill the slayer of his brother and to extinguish the heir as well, etc. — and behold, this is the very case before us. For when Judah tells his father that he is under duress and cannot save Benjamin from the king's hand, it is certain that Jacob will not be cruel and destroy them both, Judah and Benjamin, and will release Judah from his ban. Therefore he said a second argument: ״כִּי אֵיךְ אֶעֱלֶה״ etc., for he fears the danger of his father's death."], :book :genesis, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 44, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:48:44.684274903Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}